# Repo map {#repo-map}

- `spec/` — the ONE normative specification: `GEML-spec.md` (EN),
  `GEML-spec_CN.md` (中文), with `spec/in_geml_format/` holding its GEML dogfood
  copies. `spec/profiles/` holds the application-layer vocabularies
  (`geml-codemap/v1`, `geml-style/v1`, `geml-history/v1` — MIT, not the spec;
  see `spec/profiles/README.md`), `spec/proposals/` the GEPs.
- `geml-parser/` — reference parser + `geml` CLI (TypeScript; npm package
  `@geml/geml`; `server.json` is the MCP registry manifest).
- `docs/` — site and docs assets · `integrations/` — editor/tooling
  integrations · `playground/` — demos, incl. the codemap playground.
# Build, test, validate {#build-test}

=== code {lang=sh}
cd geml-parser
npm run build              # tsc -> dist/
node test/all.mjs          # full suite via the single runner (never npm-in-npm: Windows PATH overflow)
npm run coverage:check     # c8 gate: 95% lines/statements/functions/branches
node dist/geml.js check <file.geml>   # validate any GEML doc (exit 0 = clean)
===

Run an expensive suite ONCE and take output + exit code from that same run.

# Conventions {#conventions}

- Prefer the local CLI `node geml-parser/dist/geml.js` over a globally
  installed `geml` (dogfood the working tree; no PATH/shim quirks).
- Never `npm i @geml/geml` (without `-g`) inside `geml-parser` — it is a
  self-dependency.
- A parser version bump touches SIX files, not two: `geml-parser/package.json`,
  `server.json` (x2 — the MCP registry keys on it; stale value = "duplicate
  version" on publish), `package-lock.json` (x2), and the claude-plugin and
  codex-plugin manifests (the mcp suite guards those two). `PARSER_VERSION` is
  read at runtime — never hand-bump it. Add the release to `CHANGELOG.md` in
  the same commit. Three artifacts BUNDLE a copy of the parser and go stale
  silently — the committed `playground/playground.js`, `playground/codemap/`,
  and the two extensions. `docs/PUBLISHING{,_CN}.{geml,md}` has the
  dependency graph, every artifact's version files / where it lands / how it
  publishes / prerequisites / post-release checks, the order, and the traps;
  read it before cutting any release. The `.md` pair is generated from the
  `.geml` pair with `--to md` — edit the `.geml`.
- GitHub releases here are IMMUTABLE: never delete a release to fix one
  (deleting permanently burns its tag) — cut a NEW tag and
  `gh release create --latest`.
- Small changes go straight to `main`; feature branches are for large work.
- Spec/doc `.geml` edits are auto-snapshotted into their `.gemlhistory`
  (PostToolUse hook) — `geml revert <file> '#id'` rolls back one block.
  Config docs under `.claude/` are excluded: git is their history; no
  `.gemlhistory` is generated or committed there.
- `spec/in_geml_format/*.geml` are NOT pure `--to geml` output: they carry
  hand-applied fixes that regeneration would silently destroy — `../`-prefixed
  relative links (the `.geml` sits one directory below its `.md` source),
  hand-named block ids (`#code-embed-base`, `#code-source-route-cn`) in place of
  auto `#code-N`, and widened fences. Never overwrite one with a bare
  regeneration; diff against it and re-apply those fixes, or edit the block in
  place with `geml set`.
- Cross-platform: code and tests must hold on Windows (dev) AND Linux (CI) —
  path separators, CRLF, cmd.exe quoting, git identity, `fs.watch` quirks.
- Any change to `geml-parser/src/geml.ts` top-level imports or re-exports must
  be mirrored in the viewer's esbuild stubs (`integrations/geml-viewer/src/
  render-html-stub.js`, node-stub): the browser bundle aliases those modules,
  and esbuild fails the whole build on ANY missing named export (node:os hit
  4b93941; pageAssets hit cd8bed4). Run the viewer gate after touching them —
  and capture its REAL exit code (a `| tail` pipe reports tail's, not npm's).
